## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit reasoning, non-branching logic, and careful boundary handling across hi/lo halves. The value is a power of two when exactly one bit in the 128-bit space is set.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value split into hi/lo; treat as two 64-bit lanes.
- Power-of-two check: a nonzero 64-bit word is a power of two if (x & (x-1)) == 0.
- Branchless composition: the single 1 must reside either in lo (and hi == 0) or in hi (and lo == 0).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch in C-like syntax (language-agnostic semantics)
int isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  unsigned int loCase = (lo != 0) && ((lo & (lo - 1)) == 0) && (hi == 0);
  unsigned int hiCase = (hi != 0) && ((hi & (hi - 1)) == 0) && (lo == 0);
  return (loCase || hiCase) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to an embedded environment with no 64-bit ops available?
- How would you verify correctness across all 128-bit values efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design constant-time bit-manipulation without branches for 128-bit values, a common low-latency constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-reversal techniques
- 128-bit word handling
- Branchless bit tricks

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x=(x>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL | (x&0x5555555555555555ULL)<<1;
  x=(x>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL | (x&0x3333333333333333ULL)<<2;
  x=(x>>4)&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL | (x&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL)<<4;
  x=(x>>8)&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL | (x&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL)<<8;
  x=(x>>16)&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL | (x&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL)<<16;
  x=(x>>32)&0x00000000ffffffffULL | (x&0x00000000ffffffffULL)<<32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.lo); r.hi = reverse64(x.hi); uint64_t t=r.lo; r.lo=r.hi; r.hi=t; return r; }
unsigned isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x){ uint128 r = reverse128(x); return (x.hi==r.hi && x.lo==r.lo) ? 1 : 0; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to arbitrary width? 
- How does endianness affect input from memory?

